## Code style

- Follow [Effective Dart](https://dart.dev/effective-dart) and `analysis_options.yaml` (`flutter_lints`).
- Use `const` constructors where possible.
- Prefer `final` over `var`. Avoid `dynamic` unless unavoidable.
- Name files `snake_case.dart`, types `PascalCase`, members `camelCase`.
- Keep widgets small; extract reusable pieces into their own files.
- Use `package:` imports for cross-package references; relative imports within the same package.
- Prefer **sealed classes** for closed hierarchies (states, results, errors, commands).
- Prefer **pattern matching** (`switch` expressions / statements with patterns) over long `if/else` or manual type checks.

```dart
// ✅ GOOD — sealed class + exhaustive switch (LoadState lives in package:core)
Widget buildBody(LoadState<List<Item>> state) => switch (state) {
      LoadInitial() => const SizedBox.shrink(),
      LoadLoading() => const CircularProgressIndicator(),
      LoadSuccess(:final data) => ItemList(items: data),
      LoadFailure(:final failure) => ErrorView(message: failure.message),
    };

// ❌ BAD — open hierarchy with runtime type checks
abstract class LoadState {}
class LoadSuccess extends LoadState {
  LoadSuccess(this.data);
  final List<Item> data;
}
```

```dart
// ✅ GOOD — domain stays Flutter-free, returns Either
abstract class ItemRepository {
  Future<Either<Failure, List<Item>>> getItemList();
}

// ❌ BAD — throwing for expected failures
abstract class ItemRepository {
  Future<List<Item>> getItemList();
}
```

## Either for results

Use `Either<Failure, T>` from `package:core` for operations that can fail.

- **Left** — typed `Failure` (not raw strings in domain).
- **Right** — success value.
- Repositories and use cases return `Future<Either<Failure, T>>`; do not throw for expected errors.
- Cubits map to `LoadState`, keeping the **`Failure` object** on `LoadFailure` (do not collapse to a bare `String`).
